ng  people up  all over the globe that's effectively it  but it works well enough thanks to that  raw  driving force of revenge and that is  very much the crutch of the crop  character in hunt hunter in the very  beginning of the series he had a  powerful mission statement which  consisted of him saying  i do not fear death what i fear is that  my rage will one day fade away  so revenge and i guess arguably some  sort of perceived justice is like  oxygen to krapika at this stage he can't  really live without it  it's twisted it is unhealthy and it is  ultimately unsatisfactory and empty  as revenge often is and that is what  this fight against of again goes to  great  lengths to portray but i first want to  dispel any grand ideas that krapika went  into his phantom troop crusade with a  justifiable cause so obviously that  group having murdered his entire clan in  an act of genocide is quite possibly one  of the most enraging thoughts one could  have and it's very natural to be on the  side of krapika here wanting to hunt  these guys down and deliver retribution  and an  argument can be made that the phantom  troop are simply too dangerous to allow  to exist in this world and if that was  krapika's main driving force then this  would be a completely different matter  as that would be an almost noble cause  actually  using your pain to secure the betterment  of the world at large our blonde boy  korapika  is nowhere near that generous though at  the time of york new city yes retrieving  the scarlet eyes was important to him  but of equal importance was making sure  that the phantom troop were not only  removed from this world but they suffer  and ideally feel a sense of remorse just  prior to them being removed  mercilessly from existence and this is  shown very clearly in his brief  flashback with izunavi when krabika says  that there are people at large who need  to be chained to hell  and that is a very powerful and slightly  terrifying statement to make because all  of a sudden it seems like the recovery  of the scarlet eyes and laying his clan  to rest  is actually a secondary task but the  first being to inflict as  much i suppose damnation on the phantom  troop as possible  and once again this is all epitomized  within the conflict with overgain  because karapika doesn't seem to go into  this fight with the simple goal of  defeating him he wants to  impact duvagine and destroy him mentally  as well as physically  he wanted uvagine to suffer as much as  he and his clans folk had essentially  and you can see this in action as he  repeatedly gloats in front of overgain  saying stuff like  is that the best punch you've got or  going so far as to reveal his eyes and  explain his ridiculously overpowered nun  abilities or even more so pushing  uvagine to use his full power  if graphica was going into battle with  the objective of just reading of again  from the world and enacting some sort of  justice  then he would just kill overkin as  quickly as possible rather than drag out  this interaction  and yes the argument can be made that  krapika was only pushing over gin to  that 100 point in order to test out his  own limits and make sure that his nun  abilities could handle the rest of the  troop but then again that's only true in  the case of chain jail and even then it  was meant to be a purely physical test  because chain jail enforces the state of  zetsu so aura and nen  are completely irrelevant by that point  so you do need to ask yourself  why did krapika push overgain into a  state of maximum or  output with lions like stop wasting my  time use your full power  and well in my personal reading of it it  was to inflict a maximum sense of  despair upon overkeen it was to make him  feel powerless and completely outclassed  just as the members of the kurt klan had  felt when uvegan was slaughtering them  for personal profit  in essence it wasn't a sneaky cheap  victory which karapika was perfectly  capable of achieving he made sure to  allow uvagine to max himself out before  promptly smacking him back down with the  giant metaphorical hammer of court of  justice or at least that was the general  idea but did this  actually work graphica went into this  fight with what i would say was close to  a 100 percent chance of victory hoover  games fate was actually sealed the very  moment he encountered karapika a second  time  with that said i'm gonna throw in  another little twist thing here  because even with all of that in mind in  my opinion  this entire conflict was an overwhelming  defeat for karapika and a lot of this  has to do because of the criteria that  krapika himself had set i remind you of  that quote again  there are people in this world who need  to be chained to hell krapika wanted  overkin to suffer perhaps even repent  well you know what we don't even have to  go that far  he wanted uvagine to show some sort of  emotional response  to being the target of revenge he wanted  to begin to know that his prior actions  were going to cause his death  and elicit some sort of anger fear  bargaining  whatever but uvagine gave karapika  nothing but then there's also the other  side of this coin as well  we do need to assess what uvekin's goal  in this conflict was and it's not  entirely simple because it does change  throughout the battle interestingly  enough at first uvaguin is also out for  revenge he breaks out of his  imprisonment and swears to kill karapika  however just before the fight actually  begins in earnest this goal changes  because the two chat briefly and  karapika makes his own goal of revenge  clear and as such uvagine responds by  saying the following  you know what gives me the most pleasure  taking avengers like you  and smacking them down which i should  say is the viz manga translation of the  events  the official anime subtitles make the  statement even more interesting because  in that incarnation luvign says that  what he loves most in this world  is denying those who come in search of  revenge but that there is our setup  krapika wants over again to suffer for  his actions and uvagine wants to deny  krapika that satisfaction so with that  in mind how does this battle play out  well not well for grapica not very well  at all and this ranges from very simple  things like krapika trying to provoke  kuvagine into a state of rage which  simply doesn't work i mean i briefly  mentioned before that he tried to gloat  by asking overgain if that was the  strongest punch he had which did twig or  again briefly but he pretty immediately  recovered and even used the challenge to  generate a sense of excitement within  himself  for a lot of this fight he was having  the time of his life and in fact even in  some of ubergen's final moments when  kropika revealed his scarlet eyes  instead of feeling any sense of you know  oh [ __ ] luv again just remembered how  strong the curta clown was  and got even more excited despite the  fact that he was helplessly trapped  within chain jail already there was no  fear whatsoever in this man and  everything krapika was doing to him  only had the opposite effect of what was  intended and meanwhile i can't really  say that krapika was doing too well  mentally himself and actually you can  tell quite a bit about his temperament  from a prior conversation that occurred  with uvagine when he was initially  captured the spider was completely  calmed whilst krapika immediately  lost his cool and lashed out against the  defensive subagain from there to now  nothing had really changed so as much as  krapika at all times held both the  physical and tactical advantages  uvagine always held the emotional upper  hand this man  is an emotional rock he could not be  moved by anything that krapika could  throw against him meanwhile kropica's  rage only grew and grew especially when  it became clear to overgain that there  would be no escape and he just calmly  said  kill me and then there was the final  moment where croppiky used judgement  chain on overgain  in an attempt to extract information  about the other troop members and  instead of trying to salvage or even  prolong his life luvagine just said  get lost fool and embraced death denying  karapika  almost everything that he came into this  fight seeking uvagin died satisfied that  he had denied a sense of vengeance to  krapika  whatever victory there was was entirely  empty all karapica gained from it was a  bulky corpse to bury  otherwise though this was like i said  completely empty and not  only that you could even make an  argument that at the end of all of this  uvagine was the more noble combatant in  this battle and that's because as much  as he is  obviously a mass murderer in the end he  prioritized the well-beings of his  comrades over his own life for someone  who willingly took part in a genocide he  showed this bizarre sense of nobility  that all of the core troop members have  and this is a huge part of the reason  why uvagan is so highly regarded amongst  the spiders it's not just because of his  insane strength it's because of his  loyalty and camaraderie it's  absolutely twisted beyond all hell but  that's just the reality of the phantom  troop  and from their perspective uvagine would  have died as a hero perhaps even a  martyr but then when we look back at  krapika  and well what was he actually was he  some sort of noble avenger not really  because there isn't anybody who would  gain satisfaction from uvagine's death  not even krapika himself  because he failed to extract any of his  own personal desires from it the large  majority of which were rooted in  absolute darkness anyway  and that is a very clear window into  krapika's future in hunter hunter so  long as he pursues the path of revenge  every encounter is going to end in this  manner and i think that he very much  realized that by the end of york new  city and after pakunota's incidental  death to this day krapaka has not  actually killed anybody  in his quest to reclaim the scarlet eyes  although it is implied that he has  committed plenty of other  horrid actions but he has not taken a  life directly for his vendetta he has of  course killed people out of acts of  self-defense but that's a different  matter and i think that's because he  recognizes that overkin's death brought  him a grand  total of nothing and here is where i  should probably lay out some differences  in the 1999 anime incarnation  because it is quite fascinating the  concept i've gone through here is  essentially the same  except they make the idea a lot clearer  some may even say they smack you over  the head with it but with a couple of  very major differences the most profound  is that in the izunavi flashback  they give him the following piece of  dialogue even if you are successful  you'll have nothing to gain from it  you'll only be left with emptiness and  blood on your hands you don't have the  strength to carry that burden this  is your last chance let it go forget  revenge  and this is kind of well because neither  the manga nor the 2011 anime incarnation  which follows the monk more closely  has izunavi come anywhere close to  making a statement like this  and in fact he's actually fully  supportive of krapika's revenge quest  although he does attempt to restrain him  from making actions that are a bit too  hasty and self-destructive but at no  point does he deliver anything close to  this kind of noble dialogue to try and  convince cropica not to embark on his  journey and so i think this dialogue  from the 1999 version is just fleshing  out the idea for the audience a lot more  i don't think it's necessary because all  of the pieces are there to embody that  meaning though and they do go on to  highlight the idea further  because another very interesting change  is made immediately after krapika kills  overgain which is that he hunches over  and covers his face with his hand like  he's just gone into a state of 